== 4.7 Chapter 4 Practice Drill — 30 Questions

30 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 33 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [The difference between a topic and a theme is that a theme],
  [is a claim the text argues],
  [is shorter],
  [appears in the title],
  [names a character]
)

#mcq(2, [“The theme of the play is jealousy” is],
  [a claim],
  [a topic mistaken for a claim],
  [a quotation],
  [a judgement]
)

#mcq(3, [“Jealousy is what love becomes when it is asked for proof” is a claim because],
  [it uses abstract nouns],
  [it is longer],
  [it mentions love],
  [it names a mechanism and can be disputed]
)

#mcq(4, [The verb test asks you to underline the verb and check whether it],
  [is active],
  [is irregular],
  [argues rather than merely asserts existence],
  [is in the present tense]
)

#mcq(5, [The four columns of the ledger are claim, units, evidence and],
  [counter-evidence],
  [quotations],
  [page numbers],
  [biography]
)

#mcq(6, [The three units in a theme paragraph should be chosen from],
  [the establishing movement, the pressure and the cost],
  [one scene],
  [the chapters you recall best],
  [the beginning only]
)

#mcq(7, [Counter-evidence is the moment that],
  [is hardest to quote],
  [supports the claim weakly],
  [resists the claim and must be answered],
  [appears last in the text]
)

#mcq(8, [An essay with no concession sentence usually reads as],
  [balanced],
  [well organised],
  [short],
  [a prosecution with the defence absent]
)

#mcq(9, [In *Othello*, the counter-evidence to “love is destroyed by the demand for proof” is],
  [Desdemona's handkerchief],
  [Iago's plotting],
  [Cassio's drinking],
  [Othello's tenderness on arrival in Cyprus]
)

#mcq(10, [In *Faceless*, the claim that poverty is a lack of witnesses is resisted by],
  [the setting],
  [the title],
  [the ending],
  [the characters who do try to help the child]
)

#mcq(11, [“Love and trust”, “race and visibility”, “power”, “poverty” and “gender and silence” are],
  [the five ledgers the set texts share],
  [the five genres],
  [the five mark bands],
  [the five acts of a play]
)

#mcq(12, [The same theme in two different texts usually],
  [indicates the same author],
  [takes a different shape and produces a different claim],
  [means the same thing],
  [proves influence]
)

#mcq(13, [A theme answer needs three well-chosen units rather than ten because],
  [ten would take too long to read],
  [markers count words],
  [the paper forbids more],
  [three can be developed and ten can only be listed]
)

#mcq(14, [The paragraph structure recommended in this book is],
  [topic, example, topic],
  [introduction, body, conclusion],
  [claim, unit and evidence, mechanism, concession and return],
  [claim, quotation, summary, conclusion]
)

#mcq(15, [The “reading” sentence fails when it names],
  [a consequence for the audience],
  [a relation between words],
  [a mood],
  [a mechanism]
)

#mcq(16, [“Shakespeare uses repetition for emphasis” fails because],
  [repetition is not a device],
  [it is too long],
  [it names a device without saying what it does here],
  [it misquotes]
)

#mcq(17, [A theme claim that could be made about any text is],
  [required by the syllabus],
  [strong, because it is general],
  [ideal for a conclusion],
  [weak, because it is not about this text]
)

#mcq(18, [In *Native Son*, the claim that race is “invisibility plus surveillance” means],
  [Black characters are ignored],
  [the narrator is unreliable],
  [the novel has no white characters],
  [Bigger is watched constantly and recognised never]
)

#mcq(19, [In *Harvest of Corruption*, the claim that corruption is an employment agency means],
  [the play is about jobs],
  [the system recruits the people it will later consume],
  [Aloho is a civil servant],
  [the ministry is efficient]
)

#mcq(20, [A ledger is useful in the exam because it],
  [holds a claim and its evidence in a form that can be recalled],
  [replaces the text],
  [provides quotations to copy],
  [counts the pages]
)

#mcq(21, [The strongest opening for a theme answer is],
  [“The play argues that …”],
  [“This essay will discuss …”],
  [“Many critics have said …”],
  [“The theme is …”]
)

#mcq(22, [A theme question that says “discuss” wants],
  [a claim, its evidence, and its limits],
  [a list of scenes],
  [the author's biography],
  [a summary]
)

#mcq(23, [If two texts share a theme but reach opposite conclusions, the examinable point is],
  [the genre difference],
  [what each text's conclusion implies about its world],
  [the publication dates],
  [that one author copied the other]
)

#mcq(24, [The counter-evidence column protects a candidate from],
  [writing too briefly],
  [using the wrong text],
  [overstating a claim until it becomes indefensible],
  [misquoting]
)

#mcq(25, [The theme of gender in *Othello* is best stated as],
  [women suffer],
  [a woman's plain speech is not accepted as evidence],
  [marriage is difficult],
  [men are jealous]
)

#mcq(26, [In *Lonely Days*, the theme of custom is best stated as],
  [old people are cruel],
  [villages are conservative],
  [custom turns a private grief into a public procedure],
  [custom is bad]
)

#mcq(27, [A theme answer that quotes ten times and argues once is],
  [evidence-rich and argument-poor],
  [a model answer],
  [excellent],
  [too short]
)

#mcq(28, [Judging a text's theme means asking],
  [whether you agree with it],
  [how long it is],
  [whether it is popular],
  [what it costs the text to make its claim]
)

#mcq(29, [The most reliable way to find a text's themes is to ask],
  [what the ending does with the characters' wants],
  [who narrates],
  [what the title means],
  [how many chapters there are]
)

#mcq(30, [A theme ledger built for one text can be reused for another],
  [only if the texts share a genre],
  [only in poetry],
  [by keeping the columns and changing every entry],
  [without change]
)

